WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
AI governance becomes practical through policies, standards, and guardrails.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.420
These define what responsible behavior looks like in daily work.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:21.280
Guardrails may address acceptable use, restricted data types, approval requirements, model selection rules, vendor review expectations, logging requirements, human review expectations, and escalation paths.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:37.260
External resources such as NIST's AI Risk Management Framework, NIST generative AI profile material, and ISO/IEC 42001 can inform governance discussions, but organizations still need to follow their own policies and authoritative sources.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.920
Guardrails should be clear enough that teams know what is allowed, what requires review, and what is prohibited.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.860
Good governance makes responsible behavior easier.
